= Program (computing) =

== Overview ==
A '''computer program''' is an organized set of instructions, definitions, and related information intended to be interpreted or executed by a computing system. Programs are the concrete artifacts through which people specify computational behavior.

A program may be written as human-readable source code, translated into another representation such as machine code or bytecode, interpreted directly, embedded in firmware, or assembled from several languages and components.

== Definition ==
In practical computer science, a program is a representation of instructions that a computer can execute, usually together with the data definitions and structure needed to determine behavior. NIST describes computer code as instructions and data definitions expressed in a programming language or produced by an assembler, compiler, or other translator.

A program is related to, but distinct from, an [[Algorithm|algorithm]]. An algorithm is a method for solving a problem or producing a result; a program is an implementation or executable expression of computational behavior in a particular language and environment.

== Scope & importance ==
Programs connect abstract computation to real machines. They specify what operations should occur, under which conditions, using which data, interfaces, and resources.

The same algorithm can be implemented by many programs. Likewise, a single program can contain many algorithms plus user-interface logic, error handling, data models, configuration, communication, and integration code that are not well described as one algorithm.

== Core concepts ==
A program commonly involves:

* '''Instructions''' — operations to perform.
* '''Control flow''' — sequencing, branching, repetition, function calls, events, concurrency, or other execution structure.
* '''Data''' — values and representations manipulated by the program.
* '''State''' — information retained across operations.
* '''Interfaces''' — ways the program communicates with users, files, devices, networks, libraries, or other programs.
* '''Environment''' — the runtime, operating system, virtual machine, hardware, or service context in which execution occurs.
* '''Errors and exceptional conditions''' — behavior when assumptions fail or external conditions change.

== Source, translation, and execution ==
A source program is written in a [[Programming language|programming language]]. Depending on the language and implementation, it may then be:

* compiled ahead of time into machine code;
* translated into an intermediate representation or bytecode;
* interpreted during execution;
* just-in-time compiled;
* combined with libraries or other modules;
* executed inside a runtime, virtual machine, browser, operating system, database, or specialized device.

NIST defines '''runtime''' as the period during which a computer program is executing. This is useful because a program as an artifact is not identical to a running instance of that program.

== Program vs. process ==
A program is normally a stored description of behavior. A '''process''' is an executing instance with its own runtime state and resources. One program can have several processes running simultaneously, each with different inputs or state.

This distinction is central to operating systems, concurrency, distributed systems, debugging, and security.

== Examples ==
A command-line utility that reads a file and prints statistics is a program.

A web application may consist of several programs: browser-side code, server processes, background workers, database procedures, and deployment tooling.

A compiler is itself a program that translates source programs from one representation into another.

An embedded controller may run a small program continuously to read sensors and control a physical device.

== Correctness and behavior ==
Programs can be evaluated against specifications: do they produce required outputs, preserve safety properties, handle errors, respect security boundaries, and use acceptable resources?

Testing provides empirical evidence about behavior for selected cases. Static analysis, type systems, model checking, and formal verification can provide other forms of evidence. NIST's glossary describes a correctness proof as a formal technique for proving mathematically that a computer program satisfies specified requirements.

== Applications / implications ==
Programs are the basic executable artifacts of software systems. They implement applications, services, operating systems, databases, games, scientific simulations, machine-learning systems, network protocols, development tools, and embedded controllers.

Their behavior depends not only on source code but also on inputs, dependencies, configuration, runtime environment, external systems, and hardware. Reproducibility and security therefore require attention to the complete execution context rather than source text alone.

== Technical depth ==
More advanced program analysis studies semantics, control-flow and data-flow graphs, type systems, program transformations, compilation, optimization, concurrency, memory models, program synthesis, verification, and equivalence.

Programs may be deterministic or nondeterministic, terminating or long-running, sequential or concurrent, local or distributed. Some programs respond continuously to events rather than computing one final output.

== Epistemic notes ==
"Program" is an established term, but its boundary varies by platform. Scripts, stored procedures, notebooks, smart contracts, firmware images, workflows, and configuration languages may all contain executable behavior. When classification matters, describe the execution model and artifact rather than relying on the word alone.

